latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Identify all non-zero cells.\n   b. Group these cells into connected shapes.\n   c. For each shape, determine its dimensions and boundaries.\n\n2. Sort the shapes:\n   - Order the shapes from left to right based on their leftmost column.\n\n3. Calculate the total available space:\n   - Determine the width of the grid.\n   - Subtract the total width of all shapes from the grid width to get the available space.\n\n4. Distribute the available space:\n   a. Calculate the ideal space between shapes: \n      (available space) / (number of gaps between shapes + 1)\n   b. If there's only one shape, center it in the grid.\n\n5. Reposition the shapes:\n   - Start from the left of the grid.\n   - For each shape:\n     a. Calculate its new leftmost column based on:\n        - The current position in the grid\n        - The ideal space calculated earlier\n        - The size of the shape\n     b. Ensure the new position doesn't cause overlap with previous shapes.\n     c. Adjust if the shape would extend beyond the right edge of the grid.\n\n6. Create the new grid:\n   - Initialize a new grid of the same size as the input, filled with zeros.\n\n7. Fill in the new grid:\n   - For each shape:\n     a. Calculate the horizontal shift (new position - original position).\n     b. Copy each cell of the shape to its new position in the new grid.\n\n8. Return the new grid.\n\nThis plan takes into account the key observations:\n- It preserves the vertical position and internal structure of shapes.\n- It maintains the left-to-right order of shapes.\n- It aims for an optimal distribution of shapes across the horizontal space.\n- It handles edge cases like single shapes or shapes of varying sizes.\n\nThe core of this approach is in steps 4 and 5, where we calculate and apply the ideal distribution of shapes. This should create a balance between spreading out the shapes and not leaving too much empty space, addressing the nuanced behavior we observed in the test cases."
